{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1139063-domesticated-animal-spirits","id":1139063,"title":"Domesticated Animal Spirits","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-09-09T18:13:43-07:00","id":1139063,"title":"Domesticated Animal Spirits","content":"In my own game, I've run into a situation where the &quot;politics&quot; of the animal spirits have come into play. And one part of that are the spirits of the common domesticated animals - pets like dogs and cats plus the farmyard animals. I am therefore looking for people's thoughts, ideas, and comments on them.<br /> <br /> The books give few comments or descriptions on the spirits of domesticated animals. However, we do know that some of these spirits have even served as pack totems for Garou (Bull, Cockerel, Horse, Father Mule). For some species that have both wild and domesticated versions (like ducks, geese, and goats), the totem descriptions can sometimes indicate the wild or domesticated varieties, or even both.<br /> <br /> I imagine that such spirits are rare relative to the spirits of wild animals, but that they do exist. There might even be some kind of rare penumbral Domain that spawns them similar to Glens (since Domains are connected to Near Umbral realms, it is probably Wolfhome since that is the one where &quot;Man&quot; seems to rule.). I need some name for that. Maybe Pastoral? Such domains might happen in rustic farm lands where man still works closely with nature, taming it but still living with it unlike the paved over areas of the towns and cities.<br /> <br /> It is in that rural countryside area where those spirits are safe and most plentiful. They are also present in suburban environments, but are much fewer in number. A few brave ones head into the city, but urban areas of course are overrun by Weaver and Wyrm spirits and thus very dangerous.<br /> <br /> The reactions of the domesticated animal spirits are probably hostile to the Garou since their first loyalty is to their master Man. <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i>\u201cWhat do you want? What do you know about it? What are you talking about? Everything belongs to Him, just as I do. But I, I love Him. I worship Him, I serve Him. Do you think you can oppose Him, poor creatures like you? He's all-powerful. He's above all of you. Everything we have comes from Him. Everything that lives or grows comes from Him.\u201d</i><br /> \u2015 The Dog, (Bambi by Felix Salten) </div> </div> </div>The domesticated animal spirits still acknowledge Gaia, but they believe she has given Man dominion over the whole world. They are probably ambivalent to Bone Gnawers and most friendly to the Glass Walkers, but likely feel increasingly rebuffed by that tribe for ignoring them (the traditional helpers of man) in favor of truly weird Weaver spirits. I think their reactions to the Glass Walkers vary by individual, but include a lot of warnings that the tribe is ignoring them too much now. The other Garou they dislike and think are dangerous, and of course are enemies of Griffin and the Red Talons. <br /> <br /> Some exceptions must be made of course since domesticated animal totems are open to Garou. Since it is mentioned in the Player's Guides, Bull is even fairly popular, Cockerel has a following among the Fianna, and Father Mule likes metis. But overall, they are probably negative although how negative and how they react depends on the specific animal spirit. But dogs have to be the most loyal to Man, and are therefore is one of the most hated, feared, and despised animal spirit to the wild spirits.<br /> <br /> Then there are wild animal spirits which nevertheless are also friendly to Man. Spirits that live commensally among Man like the cockroach, rat, pigeon and many other kinds of birds, are probably quite friendly to the domesticated animal spirits. And we also know it can go the other way as there are spirits of feral animals as well.<br /> <br /> I haven't given this much thought before, but I think there is a lot of opportunity here for interesting stories in the umbra and with spirits since every tribe, pack, and theurge will react differently to this type of spirit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-11T03:25:59-07:00","id":1139391,"title":"","content":"Two things:<br /> <br /> \u200b1. Given that humankind has (to an extent) removed itself from having ties to the spiritual / natural world and/or (to an extent) been cut off from the spiritual world... I would think that spirits of domesticated animals would be rarer in the Umbra and even Penumbra than those of wild animals, and also weaker as well.<br /> <br /> \u200b2. I'd expect a domesticated animal spirit's relationship to humans to be less a subservient one than a symbiotic one, like that of a City Father. A City Father could not exist without the humans that are the heart and soul of the city... but even though the humans make all the critical decisions governing the city, the City Father is still to an extent an independent autonomous entity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/19/w20supporterheavymetal1.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/546/wpur.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/545/7z8m.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/125x125q90/829/8seg.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3506-uniform-two-six","name":"Uniform Two Six","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3506_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"455"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-11T12:11:45-07:00","id":1139509,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the response.<br /> <br /> I agree that such spirits are rarer, but don't see why they should be any &quot;weaker&quot; simply because they are domesticated. Individual spirits should reflect what that animal is like in the Realm. A dog spirit will be &quot;weaker&quot; compared to a wolf or cougar spirit, but will be &quot;stronger&quot; than spirits like foxes, rabbits, or even badgers. And in terms of spiritual power, these spirits seem to be able to serve as pack totems to Garou so we have no reason to think they are somehow more limited in power or in other ways than any other spirit. Usually domesticated versions of animals are weaker than the wild ones, but not always so. And certain domesticated animals like the horse probably had much of their strength and size bred back into them so that they were not obviously weaker than their wild ancestors.<br /> <br /> Well, there is a big difference between spirits of domesticated animals and a city father. Domesticated animals are actual creatures, and their spirits should reflect them. City Fathers are more like an idea or concept since they are supposed to represent the unique &quot;essence&quot; of that community. It is not &quot;real&quot; to the extent that animals are. It is an emergent property arising from collective humanity. And it is a categorically different kind of spirit than animal spirits. It is not naturae, but more like similarly aligned Weaver spirits like Sodals and Clashing Boom Boom. They will behave differently than other spirits. I don't think a comparison between domesticated animals and a Weaver concept like City Father is useful.<br /> <br /> Now there is going to be a range of attitudes towards Man by these spirits. A spirit like Bull is likely to be least subservient. As uncastrated male cattle, bulls are extremely aggressive and dangerous. I would imagine that Bull sees itself more like a partner to Man and emphasize its great strength. This is what would make it a more appropriate totem for Garou packs. But a Dog spirit should be very loyal to Man and obedient. Loyalty may not necessarily mean subservient either. A dog spirit will see Man as the Master, but in the way that Man is like the leader of the pack and an almost magical creature (I am reminded of something I recently saw that explained to Dogs, Men are like Elves) who act as powerful guardians and protectors. A dog spirit would only think it is proper and virtuous behavior to respond to such loyalty and obey Him. Dogs and cats behave very differently. A cat spirit will describe its relationship to Man very differently. They may speak like Man is a servant, but since he's so useful a cat is almost as loyal to Man as the dog. Sheep on the other hand will be very submissive.<br /> <br /> Overall, I think there needs to be a sharp line between the spirits of wild animals and domesticated animals, and that line should be in their relationship with Man. There is certainly a range of appropriate depictions in that relationship, but I think it must be much stronger than symbiotic. Lots of species can have a symbiotic relationship to man, but it doesn't make them domesticated. At the least I would go with the much stronger word &quot;loyal&quot; to Man.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-11T16:39:11-07:00","id":1139577,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1139063-domesticated-animal-spirits?p=1139509#post1139509\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thanks for the response.<br /> <br /> I agree that such spirits are rarer, but don't see why they should be any &quot;weaker&quot; simply because they are domesticated. Individual spirits should reflect what that animal is like in the Realm. A dog spirit will be &quot;weaker&quot; compared to a wolf or cougar spirit, but will be &quot;stronger&quot; than spirits like foxes, rabbits, or even badgers. And in terms of spiritual power, these spirits seem to be able to serve as pack totems to Garou so we have no reason to think they are somehow more limited in power or in other ways than any other spirit. Usually domesticated versions of animals are weaker than the wild ones, but not always so. And certain domesticated animals like the horse probably had much of their strength and size bred back into them so that they were not obviously weaker than their wild ancestors.<br /> <br /> Well, there is a big difference between spirits of domesticated animals and a city father. Domesticated animals are actual creatures, and their spirits should reflect them. City Fathers are more like an idea or concept since they are supposed to represent the unique &quot;essence&quot; of that community. It is not &quot;real&quot; to the extent that animals are. It is an emergent property arising from collective humanity. And it is a categorically different kind of spirit than animal spirits. It is not naturae, but more like similarly aligned Weaver spirits like Sodals and Clashing Boom Boom. They will behave differently than other spirits. I don't think a comparison between domesticated animals and a Weaver concept like City Father is useful.<br /> <br /> Now there is going to be a range of attitudes towards Man by these spirits. A spirit like Bull is likely to be least subservient. As uncastrated male cattle, bulls are extremely aggressive and dangerous. I would imagine that Bull sees itself more like a partner to Man and emphasize its great strength. This is what would make it a more appropriate totem for Garou packs. But a Dog spirit should be very loyal to Man and obedient. Loyalty may not necessarily mean subservient either. A dog spirit will see Man as the Master, but in the way that Man is like the leader of the pack and an almost magical creature (I am reminded of something I recently saw that explained to Dogs, Men are like Elves) who act as powerful guardians and protectors. A dog spirit would only think it is proper and virtuous behavior to respond to such loyalty and obey Him. Dogs and cats behave very differently. A cat spirit will describe its relationship to Man very differently. They may speak like Man is a servant, but since he's so useful a cat is almost as loyal to Man as the dog. Sheep on the other hand will be very submissive.<br /> <br /> Overall, I think there needs to be a sharp line between the spirits of wild animals and domesticated animals, and that line should be in their relationship with Man. There is certainly a range of appropriate depictions in that relationship, but I think it must be much stronger than symbiotic. Lots of species can have a symbiotic relationship to man, but it doesn't make them domesticated. At the least I would go with the much stronger word &quot;loyal&quot; to Man.</div> </div> </div> </div>NB: As far as cats go, the domestic cat is derived from the North African wildcat, which normally lives in communal colonies where the eldest mothers have the highest status. They'll think of humans not as servants, but as either rivals or fellow members of the colony.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-12T10:16:26-07:00","id":1139728,"title":"","content":"We already know that at the very least certain domesticated or human-altered animals can result in more Epiphling-like spirits, like Lost Dogs and Feral Psychagonea (Axis Mundi, 87-88). There could certainly be Loyal Companions, Lazy Housecats (or Crazy Cat Ladies), Lambs for the Slaughter, etc types of spirits out there that embody the common and accepted concepts.<br /> <br /> There have also been fluff write-ups about what domestication does to an animal's spirit, but the only one that comes immediately to mind was done to retcon the older edition idea that Bone Gnawers could breed with dogs and have dog-like lupus forms... so I'm hesitant to reference it in actual conversations about spirits.<br /> <br /> Ultimately I'd say just about anything CAN have a spiritual representation... the Rite of Awakening would seem to suggest that. Totems like City Father/Mother (as mentioned by U26 and Black Fox above), Easy Credit, Clashing Boom-Boom, American Dream, etc. would also seem to support the concept slightly more touched on in Mage that Humans can and do still shape the Umbra, despite their general abandonment of things &quot;spiritual&quot; (in the Werewolf sense of the word).<br /> <br /> That leaves me to guess that there ARE and SHOULD BE domesticated animal spirits. As for their power and prevalence, that's a bigger question. In the World of Darkness, has millennia of fear and persecution at the hands of wolves, and ancient buried memories of the Impergium left the human race less dog-loving than the real world? Does the WoD's sharper boundaries between city sprawl and rural country-side make ranching and farm-life a more distant and obscure notion to the urban super-majority? There are probably two answers here... the one for cannon-as-written, and the one for your own game... because everyone I've ever spoken to has their own preferred level of overlap between the WoD and reality. ('The real world, but with monsters!' vs 'Parallel reality subject to mystical and alien influences since the dawn of time')","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my mediocre WoD/Aeon/Exalted art at <a href=\"http://blackspinel.deviantart.com/\" target=\"_blank\">http://blackspinel.deviantart.com</a> ! It'll make you go &quot;Yup! That's pretty mediocre!&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12162-crusher-of-cities","name":"Crusher-of-Cities","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12162_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"70"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-14T18:01:08-07:00","id":1140371,"title":"","content":"Spirit: Doge<br /> Much Rage. Very Gnosis. Such Essence.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}